<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Catana</h1><p class="st-meta">729 BCE – 263 BCE (approx.) · capital Catana</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Chalcidian Greek city at the foot of Mount Etna, home of the lawgiver Charondas, which was twice emptied of its people by Sicilian tyrants.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Chalcidian colonists from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/naxos">Naxos</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catania">Catana</a> in 729 BCE, led by Euarchus, on the coast south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-etna">Mount Etna</a>, in a fertile plain watered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simeto">Simeto</a>. The site had a good harbour and access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicels">Sicel</a> interior.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catania">Catana</a> was known for the laws of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charondas">Charondas</a>, adopted by other Chalcidian cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. In 476 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiero-i-of-syracuse">Hieron I of Syracuse</a> moved its people to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/leontini">Leontini</a>, resettled it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorians">Dorians</a> and renamed it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-etna">Aetna</a>, celebrated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindar">Pindar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeschylus">Aeschylus</a>; the original citizens returned in 461 BCE. It served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>' base during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicilian-expedition">Sicilian Expedition</a> in 415 BCE. Its farmland and wine benefited from Etna's volcanic soil, though eruptions repeatedly struck it.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 403 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysius-i-of-syracuse">Dionysius I</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catania">Catana</a> and sold its people into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, settling Campanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> there. It recovered under later rulers and in 263 BCE was one of the first Sicilian cities taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, which carried off its sundial. Roman Catina became a prosperous town, today Catania.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/catana.jpg" alt="Catana" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Dariolp83 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Catania_vista_dall%27alto_e_il_vulcano_Etna_a_sovrastarla.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>729 BCE – 263 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Catana</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Katane, Catania (ancient)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-496&amp;focus=catana" aria-label="Catana on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5057 828 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Catana lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5377 1084L5383 1099M5377 1084L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5383 1099h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5377 1084h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5377 1084h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-496&amp;focus=catana">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

